<%#
  T-26a — one rendered report, in a dashboard box.

  --- WHAT REPLACED WHAT ---

  This partial used to be an `<iframe src=…>` pointing at the base plugin's
  `report_content_report_template_path`, plus a jQuery block that read
  `frame.contentWindow.document` to size it. Both are gone with the dependency.

  The frame is now `RedmineReporterDashboards::ReportFrame` — `srcdoc` under
  `sandbox="allow-scripts"`, i.e. an OPAQUE ORIGIN — which is the same frame the template
  editor's preview draws and the reason the auto-size script could not survive: a parent
  cannot read `contentWindow.document` across an opaque origin, and the one token that
  would let it (`allow-same-origin`) is exactly what turns the sandbox into a decoration.
  So the height is CSS (`.reporter-report-frame--widget`, with `resize: vertical` so a
  reader can drag it) rather than measured. That is a deliberate trade and it is the
  security half that wins.

  --- WHY A FAILURE IS A LINE AND NOT THE `_diagnostics` PANEL ---

  INV-5 either way: an error is never rendered AS the document. But the audience differs.
  `_diagnostics` is FR-58's full panel — code, Liquid line, engine, duration — for the
  author standing in the editor. The person looking at a dashboard is usually not that
  author, and eight rows of engine detail per box is noise they cannot act on. They get
  the headline and the correlation id, which is the one value FR-58 asks a reader to
  quote, and the full panel is on the template's own page — which this heading does NOT
  link to, deliberately: the link would need `view_reporter_dashboards_reports` checked
  per viewer, and a heading that is a link for some readers and text for others is a
  second thing to get wrong. An earlier version of this comment said "one click away",
  which described a link nobody had written.

  Degradations DO render here, through the shared partial rather than a second copy:
  "this collection was truncated" is a fact about the numbers in front of them.
%>
<h3>
  <%= widget.template.name %>

  <% if widget.query %>
    (<%= "#{widget.query.project} |" if widget.query.project %>
    <% options = (widget.query.try(:as_params) || {}) %>
    <% if widget.query.is_a?(IssueQuery) %>
      <%= link_to widget.query.name, _project_issues_path(widget.query.project, options) %>
    <% else %>
      <%= link_to widget.query.name, _time_entries_path(widget.query.project, nil, options) %>
    <% end %>)
  <% end %>

  (<%= widget.outcome.total_count %>)

  <%# Icon-only PDF link. sprite_icon MUST keep its label: on Redmine 5 it renders
      no <svg>, so without a label the link_to body is empty and Rails prints the
      href as the visible link text. The label gives the link a body (and the
      accessible name); the `icon-only` class hides that text while `icon icon-pdf`
      shows the glyph (background image on R5, SVG sprite on R6). %>
  <%= link_to reporter_dashboard_icon('application-pdf', l(:label_reporter_export_report_pdf)),
              report_pdf_reporter_project_page_path(project, tab: tab.id, block: block),
              class: 'icon icon-pdf icon-only reporter-report-pdf',
              target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener',
              title: l(:label_reporter_export_report_pdf) %>
</h3>

<%# §Findings S-14 — the narrowing is stated, never silent. Answers nil for an issue
    template and for an actor who sees every hour here, so it is asked unconditionally
    rather than behind a branch a later edit can get wrong. %>
<% if (rrd_notice = reporter_time_entry_visibility_notice(widget.template, user, project)) %>
  <p class="warning"><%= rrd_notice %></p>
<% end %>

<%= render partial: 'reporter_dashboards/templates/degradations',
           locals: { outcome: widget.outcome } %>

<% if widget.outcome.ok? %>
  <% widget.outcome.sections.each do |section| %>
    <%= reporter_report_frame(section,
                              css_class: RedmineReporterDashboards::ReportFrame::WIDGET_CHROME) %>
  <% end %>
<% else %>
  <p class="nodata reporter-report-diagnostic">
    <%= reporter_diagnostic_headline(widget.outcome.diagnostic) %>
    <%= l(:label_reporter_report_diagnostic_correlation_id) %>:
    <code><%= widget.outcome.diagnostic.correlation_id %></code>
  </p>
<% end %>
